.TH OPEN-CAPTION 1 "October 18, 2026" "open-caption" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
open-caption \- open-captioning server for community theater
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B open-caption
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-data
.IR folder ]
.RB [ \-\-http\-addr
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-https\-addr
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-level
.IR level ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-file
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-browser ]
.br
.B open-caption \-\-version
.br
.B open-caption
.RB [ \-\-assets
.IR folder ]
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.B \-\-check\-show
.I show-file
.RI [ translation ...]
.br
.B open-caption \-\-hash\-password
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B open-caption
is a single program that shows live captions on the audience's own phones, tablets and screens during a play
or musical.
One computer runs it; everyone else uses a web browser on the same network.
The same program serves three pages:
.TP
.B /admin
Setup: create a show, edit its show file, upload images, choose the look, rehearse, and share the address
(a QR code).
It asks for the admin password.
.TP
.B /operator
The operator page: the person following the show presses the space bar for each line.
It asks for the operator password (the admin password also works).
.TP
.B /follow
The follow page: a second device that only watches the show and can send nothing.
It asks for the viewer password, if one is set (\fBviewer_password\fR); the operator and admin passwords also open it.
.TP
.B /
The audience page: open to everyone on the network.
.PP
Everything about the computer and the venue is set in
.IR config.yaml
(see
.B FILES
and
.BR "SEE ALSO" ).
On the first run the program writes that file with random admin and operator passwords and prints them
once in the console, then prints the addresses to open.
Anything on the command line, or in the matching environment variable, overrides the file for that run
without changing it.
.PP
The program runs until it is interrupted.
Each option below may be written with one or two leading dashes
.RB ( \-data
or
.BR \-\-data ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Use this settings file instead of
.IR data-folder /config.yaml.
.TP
.BI \-\-data " folder"
The data folder: shows, saved sessions, the saved live position and certificates.
Overrides
.B data_dir
in the settings file.
The default is
.I open-caption
in the user's configuration folder
(on macOS
.IR ~/Library/Application\ Support/open-caption ,
on Windows
.IR %AppData%\eopen-caption ,
on Linux
.IR ~/.config/open-caption ).
.TP
.BI \-\-http\-addr " addr"
Listen for plain HTTP on this address, for example
.B :80
or
.BR 127.0.0.1:8080 .
Overrides
.BR http.addr .
If the address cannot be used the program falls back to
.B http.fallback_addr
(default
.BR :8080 )
and says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-https\-addr " addr"
Turn HTTPS on, listening on this address, for example
.BR :443 .
Overrides
.B https.enabled
and
.BR https.addr .
The certificate comes from
.BR https.cert_file ,
from the automatic certificate settings
.RB ( https.acme ),
or is a self-signed one the program makes (browsers warn about it).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " level"
How much to log:
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.B warn
or
.BR error .
Overrides
.B log.level
every time the program starts.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file " file"
Also append the log to this file.
It starts a new file at about 5 MB and keeps three old ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-network\-interfaces " list"
Use only these network interfaces (a comma-separated list of names such as Ethernet or en0,en4): only their addresses are given to the audience, listed in Setup, put in the HTTPS certificate and announced as
.BR .local .
When two used interfaces are on the same network (a cable and Wi-Fi at once) only one address is offered, the wired one, else the main connection's.
Overrides
.B network.interfaces
every time the program starts.
.TP
.B \-\-network\-restrict
Also refuse connections made to an address of an interface that is not used (the same as
.B network.restrict_listening: yes
for one run), instead of only leaving it out of the lists. Connections to this computer itself (localhost) are always served.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-browser
Do not open Setup in a browser at start-up (the same as
.B defaults.open_browser: no
for one run).
.TP
.BI \-\-check\-show " file"
Check a show file and print a report, like a lint, without starting a server or reading
.IR config.yaml .
It runs the same analysis as the show file editor in Setup: every problem with its line number, in the form
.IR file : line : severity : message
(severity is
.BR error ,
.B warning
or
.BR note ),
then a count of each.
Any further file names after the option are translations of the first file; each is checked on its own and
compared with the main file (the same number and kinds of cues).
A file name of
.B \-
reads the standard input.
Images are checked against the
.I assets
folder beside the first file, or the folder given with
.BR \-\-assets .
Put other options before
.BR \-\-check\-show .
The exit status is 0 when there are no errors, 1 when there are errors, and 2 when a file cannot be read.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
With
.BR \-\-check\-show :
warnings also make the exit status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-hash\-password
Read a password from standard input (the first line) and print it as a
.B sha256:
digest, then exit; no server starts.
The digest may be put in
.B config.yaml
as
.B admin_password
or
.B operator_password
in place of the password itself, for example
.B printf '%s' 'the password' | open-caption \-\-hash\-password
(the password is read from standard input so that it does not end up in the shell's history).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, and the DNS providers built into this program for automatic certificates, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dev
For developers only.
Enables unauthenticated
.B /dev/*
control endpoints and puts a show on air at once; the program then listens on this computer only.
Never use it at a venue.
.TP
.BI \-\-show\-file " file"
With
.BR \-\-dev :
load this show file as the active show.
.TP
.BI \-\-assets " folder"
The folder of images: for
.BR \-\-check\-show ,
instead of the
.I assets
folder beside the show file; or, with
.BR \-\-dev ,
the images for
.BR \-\-show\-file .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_CONFIG
Same as
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_DATA
Same as
.BR \-\-data .
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_HTTP_ADDR
Same as
.BR \-\-http\-addr .
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_HTTPS_ADDR
Same as
.BR \-\-https\-addr .
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_LOG_LEVEL
Same as
.BR \-\-log\-level .
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_LOG
Same as
.BR \-\-log\-file .
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_NETWORK_INTERFACES
Same as
.BR \-\-network\-interfaces .
.TP
.B OPEN_CAPTION_NETWORK_RESTRICT
Same as
.BR \-\-network\-restrict ;
set it to yes, true, on or 1.
.PP
A command-line option wins over its variable, and either wins over the settings file.
.PP
With automatic certificates the DNS provider's own settings are also read from the environment, for example
.B EASYDNS_TOKEN
and
.BR EASYDNS_KEY ,
or
.B CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN ;
a variable
.B NAME_FILE
names a file that holds the value of
.BR NAME .
They win over
.B https.acme.dns.credentials_file
and
.BR https.acme.dns.credentials .
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR data-folder /config.yaml
The settings: passwords (plain text unless written as a digest, so the file is created readable by its owner only), listening ports, HTTPS,
who may open Setup, and the defaults for a show.
Every setting is described in the configuration reference.
Setup rewrites a few keys (passwords, the
.B .local
name, the standby message, the log detail) and keeps the rest and any comments.
Other changes need the program to be restarted.
.TP
.IR data-folder /shows/ <id>/
One folder per show: the show file
.RB ( show.md ),
translations
.RB ( show.<language>.md )
and
.BR assets/ .
.TP
.IR data-folder /sessions.json ", " live.json
Saved sign-ins (stored hashed) and the saved position of the show on air, so a restart carries on where it was.
.TP
.IR data-folder /tls/
The self-signed certificate, or the certificate and account kept by automatic certificates.
.TP
.IR data-folder /standby\-logo.<ext>
The logo shown on the standby screen, if one was chosen in Setup.
.SH SIGNALS
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGHUP
(and Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Break on Windows) make the program save its position and exit with status 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, or
.B \-\-version
or
.BR \-\-help ;
with
.BR \-\-check\-show ,
the show file has no errors.
.TP
.B 1
With
.BR \-\-check\-show :
the show file has errors (or warnings, with
.BR \-\-strict ).
.TP
.B 2
With
.BR \-\-check\-show :
a file could not be read.
.TP
.B non-zero
Otherwise: the settings file has problems (all of them are printed, each naming the setting),
a port or file cannot be used, or the program was started in a way it does not allow.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start with the defaults and open Setup:
.PP
.RS
.nf
open-caption
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a settings file and data folder of your own, on port 8080, without opening a browser:
.PP
.RS
.nf
open-caption \-\-config ~/oc/config.yaml \-\-data ~/oc\-data \e
    \-\-http\-addr :8080 \-\-no\-browser
.fi
.RE
.PP
Serve HTTPS as well, and keep a log file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
open-caption \-\-https\-addr :443 \-\-log\-file /var/log/open-caption.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a show file and its Spanish translation (the exit status says whether it passed):
.PP
.RS
.nf
open-caption \-\-check\-show show.md show.es.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a build contains:
.PP
.RS
.nf
open-caption \-\-version
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Anyone who can reach the server can open the Setup and operator sign-in pages, and over plain HTTP passwords cross
the network unencrypted.
Limit them with
.B access.admin_allow
and
.B access.operator_allow
in the settings file, for example
.BR "admin_allow: [localhost]" .
Setup is already limited to the computer that runs the program by default
.RB ( "admin_allow: [localhost]" );
add the address of another computer to use Setup from it.
The viewer-only password has its own list,
.BR access.viewer_allow .
A browser on the computer that runs the program is signed in as admin without a password unless
.B access.local_auto_login
is turned off.
Setup can change these lists (Settings, Security) while the program runs.
